<title>HUNGARY: HUNGARY'S COLD SPRING DELAYS PLANTING - MET OFFICE.</title>
<headline>HUNGARY'S COLD SPRING DELAYS PLANTING - MET OFFICE.</headline>
<dateline>BUDAPEST 1997-04-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>Hungary's unusually cold weather will delay maize and potato planting and will also have an adverse effect on early fruit crops, the Hungarian Meteorological Office (OMSZ) said on Thursday.</p>
<p>&quot;Careful farmers will watch the soil temperature before starting potato and maize planting,&quot; Andras Bussay of the OMSZ's agricultural department told Reuters.</p>
<p>He added that due to the coldest spring this century, soil temperature is well below the 8 to 10 degrees Celsius needed for potato planting and the 10 to 14 degrees for maize planting.</p>
<p>Crops of early fruits such as apricot, cherry and sour cherry are also in danger, he said.</p>
<p>However, wheat and barley already in the soil are more susceptible to slower growth due to drought and late snowfalls in March, Bussay said.</p>
<p>&quot;These crops will suffer some delay because of the cold, but the dry upper soil and earlier snows probably damaged them more,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>-- Denes Albert, Budapest newsroom +36 1 327 4040</p>
</text>
